              "index": 0,
              "id": "call_IjD2sSDbeOsuMPSrHTjv4KoF",
              "function": {
                "arguments": "{\"page_summary\":\"Page text and illustrations describing alpine flora and fauna in the Alps. Plant information includes the altitudinal range where alpine plants can be found (as low as 1,400 m/4,593 ft and as high as 3,400 m/11,155 ft), adaptations of high-altitude plants (specialization such as growing in rock crevices), examples of medicinal plant species found (Origanum vulgare, Prunella vulgaris, Solanum nigrum and Urtica dioica), the impact of human interference and historical deforestation on tree cover, changes in vegetation since the mid-20th century, and the effects of ski-run construction on alpine tundra. Fauna information states that the Alps are habitat for 30,000 species of wildlife, lists animals adapted to high altitudes (alpine ibex, chamois, brown bears, voles, marmots, golden eagles and bearded vultures), altitudinal records for some animals (alpine ibex sighted up to 3,000 m/9,843 ft; marmots up to 2,700 m/8,858 ft), behavioral notes (ibex use caves and descend to feed; marmots hibernate in large groups; chamois live above the tree line), and a historical note about bears in Switzerland (last recorded bear killed in 1792 near Scheidegg by hunters from Grindelwald).\",\"keyfact\":\"The Alps are a habitat for 30,000 species of wildlife.\",\"images\":[{\"image_summary\":\"Illustration: Edelweiss (Leontopodium alpinum).\",\"keyfact\":\"Edelweiss scientific name: Leontopodium alpinum.\"},{\"image_summary\":\"Illustration: Young alpine ibex with caption noting horn size.\",\"keyfact\":\"When fully grown its horns will be about one metre wide.\"}],\"tables\":[]}",
                "name": "PageDescription"
              },
              "type": "function"
            }
          ]
        },
        "finish_reason": "tool_calls",
